The partially completed probability tree below shows the results of a survey of voting intentions of shareholders in an election for President and Vice-President of a large corporation.

\exfigure{394_q1_1}

Based on this survey, what is the probability that Jo is elected President, with either Britt or Louis as Vice-President?

solutions.latex:
Let $F$, $I$, $J$ be the people who chose French, Italian and Japanese. Work from the centre outwards, starting with $n(F\cap I\cap J)=4$:
$$\begin{aligned}F\cap I\cap J&:\ 4\\ F\cap I\text{ only}&:\ 11-4=7\\ F\cap J\text{ only}&:\ 10-4=6\\ I\cap J\text{ only}&:\ 7-4=3\\ F\text{ only}&:\ 26-7-6-4=9\\ I\text{ only}&:\ 22-7-3-4=8\\ J\text{ only}&:\ 18-6-3-4=5\\ \text{none of the three}&:\ 45-(9+7+8+6+4+3+5)=3\end{aligned}$$
\exfigure{402_q27_s1}

parts.latex:
A partially completed tree diagram is shown.

\exfigure{833_q17_1}

Complete the tree diagram and find the probability that the ice cream machine is not working on at least one of the days when he visits.

solutions.latex:
\exfigure{833_q17_s1}

The machine not working on at least one day is the complement of it working on all three days.
$$\begin{aligned} P(\overline{W} \text{ on at least one day}) &= 1 - P(WWW) \\ &= 1 - (0.4)^3 \\ &= 0.936 \end{aligned}$$
